      "album": "I Bet on Sky",
      "artist": "Dinosaur Jr.",
      "score": null,
      "url": "https://boomkat.com/products/i-bet-on-sky-2f2511a2-24df-455a-b5d2-b0a00bf5a706",
      "source": "Boomkat",
      "pub_date": "2026-08-20",
      "tags": "indie / alternative",
      "excerpt": "'I Bet on Sky' is the third Dinosaur Jr. album since the original trio – J Mascis, Lou Barlow and Murph – reformed in 2005 and it marks the band’s 10th studio album since their debut on Homestead Records in 1985. \"Back in the ‘80s, if anyone has suggested that these guys would be performing and recording at such a high level 27 years later, they would have been laughed out of the tree fort. The trio has taken everything they’ve learned from the various projects they tackled over the years, and p",
      "body": "'I Bet on Sky' is the third Dinosaur Jr. album since the original trio – J Mascis, Lou Barlow and Murph – reformed in 2005 and it marks the band’s 10th studio album since their debut on Homestead Records in 1985. \n\n\"Back in the ‘80s, if anyone has suggested that these guys would be performing and recording at such a high level 27 years later, they would have been laughed out of the tree fort.\n\nThe trio has taken everything they’ve learned from the various projects they tackled over the years, and poured it directly into their current mix. J’s guitar approaches some of its most unhinged playing here, but there’s a sense of instrumental control that matches the sweet murk of his vocals (not that he always remembers to exercise control on stage, but that’s another milieu). This is head-bobbing riff-romance at the apex. Lou’s basswork shows a lot more melodicism now as well, although his two songs on I Bet on Sky retain the jagged rhythmic edge that has so often marked his work. And Murph…well, he still pounds the drums as hard and as strong as a pro wrestler, with deceptively simple structures that manage to interweave themselves perfectly with his bandmates’ melodic explosions.\n\nAfter submerging myself in I Bet on Sky, it’s clear that the album is a true and worthy addition to the Dinosaur Jr. discography. It hews close enough to rock formalism to please the squares. Yet it is brilliantly imprinted with the trio’s magical equation, which is a gift to the rest of us. For a combo that began as anomalous fusion of hardcore punk and pop influences, Dinosaur Jr. have proven themselves to be unlikely masters of the long game.\"",
      "site_id": "boomkat",
      "crawl_status": "success",
      "type": "feature"
    },
    {
      "album": "The Sacrificial Code",
      "artist": "Kali Malone",
      "score": null,
      "url": "https://boomkat.com/products/the-sacrificial-code-16175ec0-e1f6-4c1a-9f13-960c7d37b3e8",
      "source": "Boomkat",
      "pub_date": "2026-08-20",
      "tags": "dark ambient / drone / metal",
      "excerpt": "An iconic album originally brought to life via Sweden's peerless iDEAL Recordings, Kali Malone’s 'The Sacrificial Code’ is a major work featuring almost two hours of concentrated, creeping organ pieces aligned to non traditional intonation/tuning systems. It's a stunning realisation of ideas borne out of academic and conceptual rigour, with a perception-altering quality that encourages exploration without a preordained endpoint. The Sacrificial Code’ takes a more surgical approach to the methods",
      "body": "An iconic album originally brought to life via Sweden's peerless iDEAL Recordings, Kali Malone’s 'The Sacrificial Code’ is a major work featuring almost two hours of concentrated, creeping organ pieces aligned to non traditional intonation/tuning systems. It's a stunning realisation of ideas borne out of academic and conceptual rigour, with a perception-altering quality that encourages exploration without a preordained endpoint.\n\nThe Sacrificial Code’ takes a more surgical approach to the methods first explored on last year’s ‘Organ Dirges 2016 - 2017’. Over the course of three parts performed on three different organs, Malone’s minimalist process captures a jarring precision of closeness, both on the level of the materiality of the sounds and on the level of composition.The recordings here involved careful close miking of the pipe organ in such a way as to eliminate environmental identifiers as far as possible - essentially removing the large hall reverb so inextricably linked to the instrument. The pieces were then further compositionally stripped of gestural adornments and spontaneous expressive impulse -  an approach that flows against the grain of the prevailing musical hegemony, where sound is so often manipulated, and composition often steeped in self indulgence. It echoes Steve Reich’s sentiment “..by voluntarily giving up the freedom to do whatever momentarily comes to mind, we are, as a result, free of all that momentarily comes to mind.”\n\nWith its slow, purified and seemingly austere qualities ‘The Sacrificial Code’ guides us through an almost trance-inducing process where we become vulnerable receptors for every slight movement, where every miniature shift in sound becomes magnified through stillness. As such, it’s a uniquely satisfying exercise in transcendence through self restraint and by this point inarguably a modern classic.",
      "site_id": "boomkat",
      "crawl_status": "success",
      "type": "feature"
    },
    {
      "album": "Synthesizing: Ten Ragas To A Disco Beat",
      "artist": "Charanjit Singh",
      "score": null,
      "url": "https://boomkat.com/products/synthesizing-ten-ragas-to-a-disco-beat",
      "source": "Boomkat",
      "pub_date": "2026-08-20",
      "tags": "electronic",
      "excerpt": "Not the birth of acid house, but a thrilling piece of synth raga set to brisk disco beats, Bollywood soundtrack composer Charanjit Singh’s ’82 LP is simply a landmark in its own right, and a proper ‘floor-filler to boot. Since its first reissue in 2010, Singh’s cult, early use of the TB-303 on driving machine beats has been erroneously hailed the birth of acid house. OK the formula is there, but Chris Carter’s ‘Spaces Between’ (1980) and his CTI jam ‘Dancing Ghosts’ got there a couple of years b",
      "body": "Not the birth of acid house, but a thrilling piece of synth raga set to brisk disco beats, Bollywood soundtrack composer Charanjit Singh’s ’82 LP is simply a landmark in its own right, and a proper ‘floor-filler to boot.\n\nSince its first reissue in 2010, Singh’s cult, early use of the TB-303 on driving machine beats has been erroneously hailed the birth of acid house. OK the formula is there, but Chris Carter’s ‘Spaces Between’ (1980) and his CTI jam ‘Dancing Ghosts’ got there a couple of years before Singh, at the very least, and neither really inform the genius of Phuture and co a few years later. In any event, ‘Synthesizing: Ten Ragas To A Disco Beat’ is just an incredibly fruity boost to any club, revolving 10 hypnotic, pulsating jams closer to early Irdial (say, Aqua Regia’s ‘Hot Love’) than owt from the Chi. \n\nTaking a minute away from knocking out Bollywood scores and leading his wedding band covering popular songs, Singh would land upon a stroke of genius with ‘Ten Ragas To A Disco Beat’, finding the sweetspot between prevailing hi-NRG disco and millennia of raga traditions that patently transcended the sum of their parts more than 40 years later. It sounds very much of its time, rather than the stripped back linearity of later acid house, evidenced in the florid keyboard urges of ‘Raga Bhupali’, transposing a steamy sort of club canter to somewhere even more humid.\n\nA real club evergreen, suck it and see.",
      "site_id": "boomkat",
      "crawl_status": "success",
      "type": "feature"
    },
    {
      "album": "EVEN COLDER SPRING",
      "artist": "Cities Aviv",
      "score": null,
      "url": "https://boomkat.com/products/even-colder-spring",
      "source": "Boomkat",
      "pub_date": "2026-08-20",
      "tags": "beats / hip hop",
      "excerpt": "Tighter and tidier than last year's 'Electric Chair', Cities Aviv's latest set is a psilocybin-infused dream sequence packed with fuzzy ambient interludes, loopy clouded reflections and stream-of-consciousness recitations. Gavin Mays has always been on the perimeter of any given scene or other, happy enough to draw his own landscape while everyone else churns up the same mud. And that's truer than ever on his recent run; operating on his lonesome, Mays creates music that can't be pigeonholed - i",
      "body": "Tighter and tidier than last year's 'Electric Chair', Cities Aviv's latest set is a psilocybin-infused dream sequence packed with fuzzy ambient interludes, loopy clouded reflections and stream-of-consciousness recitations. \n\n Gavin Mays has always been on the perimeter of any given scene or other, happy enough to draw his own landscape while everyone else churns up the same mud. And that's truer than ever on his recent run; operating on his lonesome, Mays creates music that can't be pigeonholed - it's barely even rap. He spits on the two-part title track, but his words dilate awkwardly over noisy, new age-esque synths and blown-out, pitch-deformed rhythms. They might not even be strictly on the beat at all, that's not the point - Mays creates a mood that he prolongs throughout 'Even Colder Spring'. The second part of the title track sounds more like Seefeel than it does MF DOOM, anyways.\n\nIt's not always easy to digest - we'll admit we struggled with 'Spinnin & Spinnin' - but 'Even Colder Spring' is surprisingly approachable compared with many of his recent releases. And what it lacks in cohesion it more than makes up for in pure eccentricity. \"Maybe Mars is bougie,\" he quips over electric piano arpeggios and feedback squeals on 'Tribeca', before breathing lullabies over slowed-down soul samples on 'Concrete Masks'.",
      "site_id": "boomkat",
      "crawl_status": "success",
      "type": "feature"
    },
    {
      "album": "Materia",
      "artist": "Julia Holter",
      "score": null,
      "url": "https://boomkat.com/products/materia-a6f9ff69-a09b-44c0-b247-e170342116b8",
      "source": "Boomkat",
      "pub_date": "2026-08-20",
      "tags": "indie / alternative",
      "excerpt": "Billed as a sequel to 2024's 'Something in the Room She Moves', 'Materia' augments and extends Julia Holter's unique vision, chopping and screwing the Hildegard von Bingen-inspired title track and swaddling it with more psychotropic chamber pop experiments. Dizzier and more surreal than the original album, it might be even more dazzling - properly vital FFO Mabe Fratti, Jessica Pratt, ANOHNI or Jenny Hval. Ever since we got snagged by 2011's AOTY 'Tragedy', we've been captivated by her particula",
      "body": "Billed as a sequel to 2024's 'Something in the Room She Moves', 'Materia' augments and extends Julia Holter's unique vision, chopping and screwing the Hildegard von Bingen-inspired title track and swaddling it with more psychotropic chamber pop experiments. Dizzier and more surreal than the original album, it might be even more dazzling - properly vital FFO Mabe Fratti, Jessica Pratt, ANOHNI or Jenny Hval.\n\nEver since we got snagged by 2011's AOTY 'Tragedy', we've been captivated by her particular fusion of baroque pop, high drama and groggy minimalism - elements that inevitably arrive tied together with an air of effortless avant charm. And she's only refined her approach over the years, delivering a Laurel Canyon-via-Dusseldorf labyrinth of sophisticated juxtapositions on her last album. It was clearly so knotty, in fact, that Holter felt the need to revisit its serene centerpiece 'Materia', rolling out two new versions that triggered this heady, casually brilliant companion album. The rework that started it all, 'Materia 2', isn't some George Lucas affair, either; Holter goes back to the chilly original and genuinely reimagines it, adding a decelerated hypnagogic drum machine pulse that's one part AFX's 'Selected Ambient Works Volume II' and one part Antena's perennial classic 'Camino del Sol'. Holter never loses sight of the initial mystical inspiration, retaining the celestial ecstasy with Laswell-style fretless electric bass and soaring keys as she sings \"My heart is loud.\"\n\nThe third version of 'Materia' is more of a bonus track (a self-conscious homage to those mysterious unlisted extras that often appeared on CDs) and finds the divine in languidness, pulling down the tempo and letting the words and harmonies sound out into the empty, reverberant space. But both of these tracks work more like anchors for what's essentially a brand new album; the jerky, 'Hounds of Love'-esque opener 'The Laugh is in the Eyes' is already one of Holter's most inviting, lavish productions. Led by its cannily sculpted rhythm section at first, it flourishes when Holter lets the aesthetic tumult disappear altogether, singing out coolly over weightless pads. And that AFX-cum-Antena influence isn't relegated to the titular redux. The album's lead single 'Fantasy' is an art-pop miracle that sounds as if she's daydreaming over a demo that was passed between Rephlex and Les Disques du Crepuscule before it was lost in the mail some time in the early '90s. It doesn't rest on its laurels either, exploding outwards and somehow integrating tape saturated Walker Brothers-esque psych pop before snapping right back into the groove.\n\nThere's a pause for breath on the brief, voice-led 'Clepsydra' before Holter dives headfirst into the extended 'My Twin', the track where she pinched the lead riff she used on 'Fantasy'. When we hear that familiar refrain emerge from near silence, played on an orchestra of broken music boxes and mussed with disorienting pitch and tape delay effects, it's as if we're being scrubbed through someone else's dream. It's one of those tracks that reaffirms why Holter's so good at what she does, composed from a tangled web of ideas and references and sounding somehow effortless at the same time. 'Materia' is Holter at her most whimsical - and most vital.",
      "site_id": "boomkat",
      "crawl_status": "success",
      "type": "feature"
    },
    {
