    {
      "album": "Pareidolia",
      "artist": "Jake Muir",
      "score": null,
      "url": "https://boomkat.com/products/pareidolia-2906628f-a7f9-4299-af12-8a2285ea16f0",
      "source": "Boomkat",
      "pub_date": "2026-08-20",
      "tags": "dark ambient / drone / metal",
      "excerpt": "Jake Muir returns to enmossed with an album of \"all goop’d up ambient bits from black and death metal records...\", stirring together an atmospheric, bathhouse-ready reaction to the most extreme soundscapes, enhancing lysergic moments to bring out parallels with gear from Coil, :zoviet*france:, Pinkcourtesyphone, Main or even Ulver. What makes music heavy, exactly? Is it simply the volume itself, or could it be the spread of culturally assured elements that make our skin prickle and our ears focu",
      "body": "Jake Muir returns to enmossed with an album of \"all goop’d up ambient bits from black and death metal records...\", stirring together an atmospheric, bathhouse-ready reaction to the most extreme soundscapes, enhancing lysergic moments to bring out parallels with gear from Coil, :zoviet*france:, Pinkcourtesyphone, Main or even Ulver.\n\nWhat makes music heavy, exactly? Is it simply the volume itself, or could it be the spread of culturally assured elements that make our skin prickle and our ears focus in on fight or flight terrors like animalistic cries, rattling chains or grim liturgical chants? Muir has been fascinated with Rock music since he was a kid, poring over byzantine, maximal offerings from bands like Meshuggah and Nine Inch Nails. And it's an obsession that's led him through the bullrushes of death and black metal, genres that often veer into experimental territory, crossing streams with industrial, noise and, of course, dark ambient. 'Pareidolia' is Muir's celebration of metal's nightmarish atmospheres, sounds that make the blast beats, hoarse vocals and smudgy chords sound even more substantial without technically being loud. \n\nPulled away from their context, these textures are compellingly theatrical: whooshing ice winds, creaking branches, scraping metal, chiming bells, plucked strings and heaving drones. They're sounds that situate us in the scene’s ancestral Northern homeland, in some remote cabin hemmed in by harsh weather and prowling wolves. Heard on their own, it's predictable that we end up trudging similarly cinematic territory as the \"dark ambient\" forebears like Lustmord and Deathprod or more recent exponents Svarte Greiner and Lussuria. Muir's idea for the album was a little different; spurred on by his back-and-forth with Even Caminiti on 2022's ace 'Talisman' album, he attempted to use these bleak fantasies to power the kind of heady, sexually-charged moods that goosebump'd thru his breakout 'Bathhouse Blues' without losing the folk horror cogitation of last year's sacred meditation 'Campana Sonans'.\n\nIn fact, 'Pareidolia' is supposed to play like an illusion - even the name of the album is a reference to these phantom arrangements. Muir wants to rope us in with familiarity before waltzing us through a parallel history of isolationist, industrial ambient gear, the kind of guitar-muddling, metal-aware grot that linked Nocturnal Emissions and Z'EV and Robert Hampson's electroacoustic rock duo Main with heady set texts from Eno, Daniel Lanois, Stuart Dempster and Steve Roach. The album isn't just a collage of the quiet moments from recent RYM metal side-quests, as you'll hear as soon as you plug into opener 'Beithir'. Between the blustery soundscapes and knife-sharpening SFX, there's a melodic pulse and echoing oscillations that wouldn't sound out of place on an IASOS record, while the dense, Sunn O)))-adjacent bass drone takes on a surprisingly humid character, winding itself around weightless pads and spirit breaths.\n\nIt's the air that's heavy, not the music itself.",
      "site_id": "boomkat",
      "crawl_status": "success",
      "type": "feature"
    },
    {
      "album": "Glass Torso",
      "artist": "Joe Fujinoki",
      "score": null,
      "url": "https://boomkat.com/products/glass-torso",
      "source": "Boomkat",
      "pub_date": "2026-08-20",
      "tags": "electronic",
      "excerpt": "Japan’s Joe Fujinoki makes a sort of dungeon variant of dub techno, sounding something like the far fringes of the Chain Reaction label if it were excavated and screwed by Aaron Dilloway. Trust it’s completely off the rails, pulsing with life and landing somewhere between Vainqueur, Tolerance, AOKI takamasa, Rashad Becker, Pole. There's clearly enough life left in the motions and aesthetics of Dub Techno (and its \"ambient\" progeny) that every so often, someone digs out the defibrillator and shoc",
      "body": "Japan’s Joe Fujinoki makes a sort of dungeon variant of dub techno, sounding something like the far fringes of the Chain Reaction label if it were excavated and screwed by Aaron Dilloway. Trust it’s completely off the rails, pulsing with life and landing somewhere between Vainqueur, Tolerance, AOKI takamasa, Rashad Becker, Pole.\n\nThere's clearly enough life left in the motions and aesthetics of Dub Techno (and its \"ambient\" progeny) that every so often, someone digs out the defibrillator and shocks it back to life. This time, the paddles are handled by Tokyo-based operative Joe Fujinoki, who retains dub's latent eccentricity - the kinda unsteady, obstreperous genius that runs thru everything from Lee Perry to Basic Channel - while steering the chaos into murky new territory. Inspired by \"the fragility of the human body\", Fujinoki extends the potential of his arsenal of analog instruments by mimicking the fluid movements of the natural world, creating a circulatory system of all-electronic sequences that feels peculiarly animate, its rhythms falling in-and-out of time like heartbeats and its tones dipping and winding like snatched breaths. \n\nRather than sculpting his stack of oscillators into a familiarly fluttering nostalgia trigger, Fujinoki zeroes in on their most unstable tendencies, following a path laid out by Stefan Betke on those stone-cold-classic early Pole records, glitch milestones that emerged from the ashes of his broken Waldorf 4-Pole filter, and of course Vladislav Delay’s curving of uneven basslines and rickety clicks and pops through his loose lattice of echoes. Just peep 'Sculptural Dub I' and you'll see know what we're on about, shaping its low end into wormy spirals that inch around ping-ponging metallic smacks that wouldn't sound out of place on one of Torsten Pröfrock's most enigmatic libations.\n\n'Limb' starts off as if it's gonna launch into a Basic Channel tribute, with distant, aching pads and a 'Lyot Rmx'-style echoed stab and quickly oozes towards total abstraction, pegging its harmonic elements to scratchy rhythmic clusters and gruff interferences. At times, 'Glass Torso' plays more like a DIY noise record, the kind of off-piste synth vs tape experiment you'd expect to find on iDEAL, or rediscovered for a timeline-distorting anthology reissue, like that stone-cold-killer Tolerance box set from a few years back. Check 'Hollow', with its blood-curdling dissonance and ghoulish pseudo-generative gurgles, or the waterlogged, freewheeling rhythms that anchor the album's euphoric closing track 'Still' and tell us it ain't special. You know what to do!",
      "site_id": "boomkat",
      "crawl_status": "success",
      "type": "feature"
    },
    {
      "album": "Bad zen",
      "artist": "Frances Chang",
      "score": null,
